{"context":"**MVP scope clarification:** Adapter registration is metadata-only in MVP. Runtime does not discover, load, or execute adapters, and MVP diagnostics remain Runtime-generic. The executable adapter model described by this ADR is a post-MVP capability and, when implemented, uses standalone subprocesses through the Process Runner rather than in-process Runtime execution.\n\nAnvil is designed to serve multiple application frameworks — Laravel, Rails, Django, Express, Go, and others — without embedding framework-specific behavior in its Core. This is the principle of framework agnosticism.\n\nWithout an explicit extension mechanism, framework behavior inevitably enters the Core:\n\n**Convenience becomes coupling.** A command that is useful for the first consumer project — \"run database migrations duri Source: ADR-009-framework-adapter-architecture.md Date:2026-07-19 Status:**Superseded**.","decision":"Anvil adopts a Framework Adapter Architecture for all framework integration.\n\nFramework Adapters are the exclusive mechanism for introducing framework-specific behavior into Anvil's execution flows.\n\n- **Core** defines contracts and lifecycle semantics\n- **Framework Adapters** implement framework-specific behavior using those contracts\n- **Applications** configure and invoke Adapters as needed\n- **Core never depends on Framework Adapters**\n\nFramework behavior always remains external to the Core. No framework-specific code, convention, configuration, or logic MAY be added directly to the Core.","consequences":"### 11.1 Positive Consequences\n\n**Framework independence.** The Core contains no framework-specific code. It can serve any framework — existing ones and future ones — without modification.\n\n**Stable Core.** The Core evolves based on platform requirements, not framework-specific needs. Its stability is not affected by changes in any framework's ecosystem.\n\n**Reusable platform.** The same Core installation serves multiple projects using different frameworks. Each project uses only the Adapters it needs.\n\n**Extensible architecture.** New framework support is added by creating a new Adapter, not by modifying the Core. The extension model enables community contributions without forking the platform.\n\n**Reduced coupling.** Adapters are decoupled from each other and from the Core. Changes to one ","alternativesConsidered":"Considered\n\n### 12.1 Framework Code Inside Core\n\n**Description:** Framework-specific behavior is implemented directly in the Core. The Core ships with built-in support for the most popular frameworks. Framework-agnosticism is maintained by making all frameworks \"supported\" rather than \"assumed.\"\n\n**Rejected because:** This approach inevitably privileges the first framework. Built-in support shapes the Core's abstractions, configuration, and defaults around that framework's conventions. Other fra"}